  { q: 'What is the Corvus constellation?', a: 'Corvus is a small constellation of the southern sky representing a crow or raven. In Greek myth, it is associated with Apollo\'s raven. The constellation contains four main stars in a distinctive quadrilateral pattern, and is located near Hydra (the water snake) and Crater (the cup). The mythological story connected to Corvus involves Apollo sending his crow/raven to fetch water in a cup (crater); the bird delayed to eat figs and then blamed a water snake for blocking the spring. Apollo saw through the lie and placed the crow, the cup, and the snake in the sky as a constellation grouping — with the crow unable to reach the water cup as eternal punishment.' },
